In this study, we benchmark two models for Estonian text simplification. The first model is based on neural machine translation, treating ATS as a translation task. The second model involves fine-tuning the LLaMA language model on the Estonian Simplification Dataset. While standard metrics such as BLEU and SARI are often used to evaluate text simplification models, they do not capture essential aspects like readability and meaning preservation. This study combines these metrics with manual evaluations to provide a comprehensive assessment
The structure of the paper is as follows: Section 2 describes the creation of the Estonian Simplification Dataset. Section 3 replicates an English-language experiment [23] and provides the rationale for selecting OpenNMT as the baseline for Estonian ATS benchmarking. Section 4 covers the fine-tuning of LLaMA on the Estonian dataset with optimization using the Unsloth library. The final two sections present the evaluation results for both models and present the conclusions.
2 Building the Estonian Simplification Dataset
In this section, we outline the process of constructing the Estonian Simplification Dataset. The primary goal is to create a sufficiently large dataset to effectively train deep neural network models. Given the required scale and the lack of any existing simplification dataset for Estonian, it is clear that manually compiling this dataset would be impractical.
Where possible, the dataset is aligned with simplified English sentences, and we also aim to establish guidelines to capture linguistic phenomena specific to Estonian, suitable for simplification.
The first source for our dataset is the Turk corpus [22], which contains 2,359 original Wikipedia sentences, each paired with eight simplified versions created via Amazon Mechanical Turk. This corpus provides a variety of simplification styles, including lexical substitution and sentence rephrasing. Since it is widely used in text simplification research, we manually translated relevant portions into Estonian, focusing on sentences that preserved a simplification relationship.
The second source is Wikipedia Data Set 2.0 [7], which contains 167,689 aligned Wikipedia sentences. Small samples from this dataset were translated using machine translation and then corrected by Estonian annotators. However, the dataset ultimately proved unreliable, as many sentences lack coherence.
To ensure consistency and reproducibility of simplification techniques, we developed the Estonian Simplification Guidelines, inspired in part by the Newsela Corpus [21]. The Newsela Corpus includes 1,130 news articles, each rewritten at four different levels to cater to readers of varying abilities, with professional editors ensuring quality and readability. Although the corpus is not freely available, it can be accessed for academic or research purposes.
Our Estonian Simplification Guidelines consist of a detailed 10-page document outlining specific simplification techniques tailored to Estonian. These guidelines focus on three main areas:
• Grammatical complexity: Simplifying cases, tenses, and grammatical moods.
• Syntactic complexity: Reducing sentence length, splitting long sentences, and rearranging word order for clarity.
• Lexical simplification: Replacing complex words with simpler synonyms or broader terms to improve comprehension.
Following the approach used in English-language research, the primary source for Estonian simplification data is Estonian Wikipedia. Articles are downloaded from the latest dumps, split into individual sentences, and only sentences with more than 15 words are selected for simplification. To efficiently generate many simplified sentences, we employed GPT-4.0 [11], a state-of-the-art language model with a high ranking on the Chatbot Arena LLM Leaderboard. Although generating data with GPT-4.0 incurs costs, they are significantly lower than those associated with human simplification.
The model, pretrained on extensive datasets, can be fine-tuned to adopt specific personas, enabling it to emulate professional tones and styles tailored to various roles, such as technical support or educational tasks. Studies have shown that persona-based prompting techniques outperform other prompting methods and are widely adopted in practice [13, 20].
In our research, we developed a variety of prompting templates incorporating different simplification strategies. These templates were tested across various instances of GPT-4.0. The initial templates did not incorporate any persona style, while later versions introduced persona agents focused on syntactic and lexical simplification. After experimentation, we found that the best results were achieved by first applying lexical simplification, followed by syntactic simplification. Although recent research has raised questions about the effectiveness of persona-based prompting [25], we found that, for this specific task, agent prompts are indeed more effective.
