(Seq2Seq) models and transformer architectures—can be adapted to translate complex sentences into simpler forms within the same language.
One foundational architecture for text simplification [10], capable of lexical simplification and content reduction, is based on the OpenNMT framework. OpenNMT [8] uses a Seq2Seq architecture with an Encoder-Decoder structure, typically utilizing Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) such as LSTMs or GRUs. The Attention Mechanism in OpenNMT allows the model to focus on relevant parts of the input, improving the quality of the simplified output.
The tested MT simplification uses OpenNMT-py, built on PyTorch, for flexibility and integration with modern deep learning tools.
The model architecture includes:
• 2 layers with 500 hidden units per layer,
• A 0.3 dropout rate to prevent overfitting,
• Training over 15 epochs using the Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) optimizer with a 0.1 learning rate.
The DRESS model [23] is a sentence simplification algorithm based on reinforcement learning. Unlike traditional supervised learning models, DRESS optimizes simplification by continuously updating itself through feedback, aiming to maximize the quality of the simplified output.
The model utilizes a pretrained encoder-decoder architecture, where the encoder processes the complex input sentence and generates a latent representation, while the decoder generates a simpler version of the sentence. Reinforcement learning is applied to fine-tune this process, using a reward function that encourages the model to balance grammatical correctness, meaning preservation, and simplification. By optimizing the rewards, DRESS can iteratively improve the quality of the generated simplified sentences.
This reinforcement learning approach makes DRESS more adaptive, allowing it to refine its simplifications over time by leveraging feedback from the reward system.
The last model for English text simplification involves fine-tuning the T5 (Text-To-Text Transfer Transformer) model [16]. The T5 model uses a unified text-to-text framework, treating all NLP tasks as text generation problems. It leverages transfer learning by pre-training on the C4 dataset [15] and fine-tuning on specific tasks, enhancing its generalization capabilities. T5 follows an encoder-decoder architecture, based on the original transformer model.
The T5 model is fine-tuned over 5 epochs using the Adam optimizer. The model uses its own tokenizer and preprocesses the data with control tokens that guide the simplification process. These tokens, such as character length ratio and Levenshtein distance, help improve output quality.
The data is fed to the model with the "simplify" instruction, enabling T5 to generate simplified text based on its pre-trained knowledge and the task-specific fine-tuning.
The above models were trained on WikiSmall [26], a benchmark corpus containing aligned complex and simple sentences from both standard and Simple English Wikipedias. The training set consists of 89,042 pairs of complex and simplified sentences, while the test set includes 100 sentence pairs.
The evaluation of the three models was conducted using the following metrics:
• BLEU (Bilingual Evaluation Understudy Score) [12]: Measures the accuracy of machine-generated text by comparing it to reference translations based on the overlap of n-grams. It does not award deletion, but it is a good metric to measure how similar the machine-generated text is to the reference sentences.
• SARI (System Output Against References and Input) [22]: It is a metric meant purely to measure text simplicity. Evaluates text simplification by comparing system-generated text with both the original input and simplified references, rewarding good edits and penalizing unnecessary changes.
• FKGL (Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level) [6]: Assesses the readability of a text by estimating the U.S. school grade level required to understand it. It is calculated based on word count in a sentence and syllable count in words. It can give valuable info about occurrence of sentence splitting and word substitution.
