5.1 Automatic Evaluation
Neural machine translation algorithms like OpenNMT, used for text simplification, require more training data than is currently available in the Estonian Simplification Dataset.
To enhance the performance of OpenNMT for the Estonian language, the English Wiki-Small Corpus has been automatically translated into Estonian using the Tartu NLP MT service [9].
Llama 3.0 and Llama 3.1 were qualitatively evaluated by fine-tuning them on the Estonian Simplification Dataset, excluding the gold standard, and applying them to simplify the original sentences from the gold standard. Llama 3.1 demonstrated slightly better performance and was therefore selected for the final evaluation.
Figure 2: Comparison of Model Performance on BLEU, SARI, and FKGL Metrics for Llama 3.1 and OpenNMT
As in the English case, training was conducted with the OpenNMT-py library, using the same parameters except for an increased vocabulary size of 60,000 with vocabulary sharing enabled. Vocabulary sharing allowed better handling of tokens unique to the input set. During training, 12 checkpoints were saved, all later evaluated to select the bestperforming model and prevent overfitting. The fine tuning parameters for fine-tuning LLaMA 3.1 were configured as follows: a batch size of 8 per device with gradient accumulation over 2 steps, and a total of 500 training steps with a warmup of 100 steps. The learning rate was set to 0.00005, using the AdamW optimizer in 8-bit mode and cosine learning rate scheduling. Weight decay was applied at 0.01 to regularize the model, with mixed precision enabled via FP16. Logging occurred every 50 steps, and a fixed seed of 42 ensured reproducibility. The pretrained model and tokenizer have been serialized for use in simplifying the sentences in the test set.
The figure 2 compares Llama 3.1 and OpenNMT models based on BLEU, SARI, and FKGL scores. OpenNMT achieves a slightly higher BLEU score (30.05 vs. 27.04), indicating closer alignment with reference texts, but Llama 3.1 performs better on SARI (49.72 vs. 47.43), suggesting it produces more effective simplifications by adding, deleting, or modifying content to improve readability. Llama 3.1 also has a marginally lower FKGL score (8.71 vs. 9.02), implying its outputs may be slightly easier to read. The automatic comparison reveals no clear winner, as both algorithms show similar performance based on standard metrics. Therefore, we should turn to a manual comparison for a definitive result.
5.2 Manual Evaluation
Three native Estonian speakers with linguistic expertise have developed the following criteria for manually rating the simplified sentences.
Grammaticality (G) 0-4 This criterion evaluates the grammatical correctness of the text, ensuring it is free from grammatical errors. The scoring system is as follows:
• 0: The text contains numerous grammatical mistakes, making it unreadable.
• 1: There are significant grammatical errors, making the sentence difficult to understand.
• 2: A few errors are present but do not heavily hinder understanding.
• 3: Minor grammatical mistakes that do not affect understanding.
• 4: The grammar is perfect, with no mistakes.
