Readability (R) 0-4 This criterion assesses how easy the text is to read and understand, as well as its naturalness and flow. Consideration is given to sentence length, word complexity, and overall coherence.
• 0: The text is completely incoherent and unreadable.
• 1: It is very difficult to read and understand.
• 2: The text is readable but requires significant effort.
• 3: The text is mostly coherent, with minor effort required to understand.
• 4: The text is very easy to read and understand, flowing naturally.
Preservation of Meaning (M) 0-4 This criterion evaluates how well the simplified text retains the original meaning and important information.
• 0: The meaning is completely lost, with significant changes in the text.
• 1: The meaning is poorly preserved, with significant loss of information.
• 2: The meaning is somewhat preserved, but important details are missing.
• 3: The meaning is mostly preserved, with only minor information loss.
• 4: The meaning is fully preserved, with no loss of key information.
Simplification (S) 0-4 This criterion evaluates how effectively the text has been simplified. It considers whether the text is shorter, uses simpler verbs, and removes unnecessary information.
• 0: The text is not simplified or is more difficult to understand.
• 1: Simplification is poor, with only slight improvement in ease of understanding.
• 2: The text is somewhat easier to understand.
• 3: Good simplification, making the text easier to understand.
• 4: Excellent simplification, with the text significantly easier to understand while retaining meaning.
The evaluations were conducted by two native Estonian linguists, who manually reviewed a set of 50 simplified sentences, with results shown in Table 3. The scores reflect a consensus, as both evaluators reached agreement on each rating. The results indicate that Llama 3.1 consistently outperforms OpenNMT across all criteria, particularly excelling in Preservation of Meaning (3.24 vs. 1.76) and Simplification (2.16 vs. 0.94), both critical for effective text simplification. In contrast, OpenNMT frequently struggled to preserve the original meaning, often omitting key terms, introducing unrelated words, or making unsuitable substitutions during lexical simplification, resulting in incomplete and incoherent sentences.
